report_text = """[AI-Powered Fraud Detection and Risk Analysis System1. AbstractFinancial fraud detection is a critical machine learning application; fraudulent transactions lead to direct financial loss, customer dissatisfaction, and elevated security risks. This project develops an AI-powered fraud detection system that analyzes transaction-level information to predict the likelihood of a transaction being fraudulent. Utilizing a synthetic dataset of 50,000 transactions, the system evaluates features such as transaction amount, account balance, device type, location, and previous fraudulent activity. The end-to-end pipeline encompasses data preprocessing, exploratory data analysis (EDA), feature engineering, handling class imbalance, baseline modeling, and XGBoost model optimization. The final deployed system utilizes an optimized classification threshold to provide a fraud probability, a stratified risk level, and a definitive fraud decision via a FastAPI backend, an interactive dashboard, and automated email alerts.2. Introduction & Problem StatementFraudulent financial transactions are difficult to detect using static business rules (e.g., IF amount > ₹100,000 THEN suspicious) because fraudulent behavior varies dynamically across transaction amounts, locations, and user habits. Traditional rule-based systems generate excessive false positives, flagging legitimate high-value transactions.The objective of this project is to develop a data-driven machine learning system capable of identifying potentially fraudulent transactions by estimating a probabilistic score ("How likely is this transaction to be fraudulent?").Primary Objectives:Analyze transaction data and identify patterns associated with fraud.Perform robust preprocessing, categorical encoding, and feature engineering.Address moderate class imbalance (32.13% fraud rate).Train baseline models and an optimized XGBoost classifier.Optimize the classification threshold based on cost-sensitive business metrics.Deploy the model via FastAPI, coupled with an interactive Streamlit dashboard and SMTP email alerts.3. Dataset Description & FeaturesThe project utilizes synthetic_fraud_dataset1.csv, containing approximately 50,000 rows and 14 columns spanning transactions from January 1, 2023, to December 31, 2023. There are approximately 8,963 unique users, with 7,317 exhibiting both fraudulent and safe transactions, indicating that fraud is not isolated to a small cluster of accounts.Target Variable: Fraud_Label (0 = Non-Fraud [67.87%], 1 = Fraud [32.13%])Feature Overview:Numerical: Transaction_Amount, Account_Balance, Daily_Transaction_Count, Card_AgeCategorical: Transaction_Type, Device_Type, Location, Merchant_Category, Card_Type (Encoded via OneHotEncoder)Binary: Previous_Fraudulent_ActivityDatetime: Date (Used for feature extraction)Identifiers (Excluded from modeling): Transaction_ID, User_ID4. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) & Feature EngineeringEDA focused on evaluating the relationship between transaction characteristics and the Fraud_Label. High transaction amounts were compared against non-fraudulent equivalents, revealing that amount alone is not a strict indicator of fraud.To provide the model with richer context, the following features were engineered:Transaction-to-Balance Ratio: Transaction_Amount / Account_Balance. Indicates the significance of the transaction relative to available funds.High Amount Indicator (is_high_amount): A binary flag identifying transactions above a learned high-value threshold.Temporal Features: txn_day_of_week, txn_month, and is_weekend (extracted from the raw Date string).5. Model Development & TrainingThe data was split chronologically or randomly into Training (34,999), Validation (7,501), and Testing (7,500) sets. Identifiers and the target label were removed from the input features ($X$).Addressing Class Imbalance & Evaluation MetricsDue to the dataset's imbalance, accuracy is a misleading metric. The project prioritizes:Recall ($TP / (TP + FN)$): Minimizes False Negatives (missed fraud), which carry a high financial cost.Precision ($TP / (TP + FP)$): Minimizes False Positives, reducing customer friction from false alarms.PR-AUC (Precision-Recall Area Under Curve): The primary optimization metric, as it effectively summarizes the precision-recall trade-off for imbalanced data.XGBoost & Hyperparameter TuningBaseline models (Logistic Regression, Random Forest) were established before training the primary XGBoost model. XGBoost—a gradient boosting algorithm highly effective for tabular data and non-linear relationships—was tuned using Optuna. Hyperparameters such as n_estimators, max_depth, learning_rate, and subsample were optimized to maximize the validation PR-AUC.Threshold Optimization & Risk LevelsStandard binary classification uses a default 0.50 threshold. However, to minimize the business cost of missed fraud (where False Negatives cost 10x more than False Positives), the decision threshold was optimized to 0.20.The system additionally classifies transactions into operational Risk Levels:LOW: Probability < 30%MEDIUM: Probability 30% – 70%HIGH: Probability > 70%Example: A transaction with a 49.66% fraud probability triggers a FRAUD decision (49.66% $\ge$ 20% threshold) but is flagged as MEDIUM RISK (49.66% $<$ 70%).6. System Architecture & DeploymentThe model is saved via Joblib and deployed as a production-ready application using the following architecture:Web Dashboard (Streamlit/HTML/JS): User interface for inputting transaction details.FastAPI Backend: Receives data, applies the saved Feature Engineering & XGBoost pipeline, and returns the Probability, Decision, and Risk Level.SMTP Alerting: Automatically dispatches an email notification if a transaction breaches the fraud threshold.A secondary ML Insights Dashboard is implemented to visualize dataset overviews, fraud distributions, feature insights, and model performance metrics (Confusion Matrix, PR-AUC) for business stakeholders.7. Future ImprovementsReal-time Transaction Streaming: Integrate Apache Kafka or AWS Kinesis to process events in real-time.User Behavioral Modeling: Develop rolling customer-level features (e.g., average transaction amount over 30 days, unusual spending patterns).Time-Based Validation: Transition from random splitting to out-of-time validation (Past $\rightarrow$ Train, Present $\rightarrow$ Validation, Future $\rightarrow$ Test) to better simulate production data drift.Explainable AI (XAI): Integrate SHAP or LIME to provide interpretable feature importance for every individual transaction, explaining exactly why a transaction was flagged to fraud analysts.Analysis of the Provided Text (Error & Gap Check)The text you pasted is a highly structured, accurate, and robust outline for an ML project. I corrected and smoothed over a few minor structural gaps during the write-up:Incomplete Sentence: The original text ended abruptly at "4. Explainable AI". I completed this thought in Section 7 by adding the context of using SHAP/LIME for model interpretability.Data Leakage Warning: In Section 13, you mention creating an is_high_amount feature based on a "learned high-value threshold." In practice, ensure this threshold is calculated only on your Training set and then applied to your Validation/Test sets. If you calculate the average/high amount across the entire dataset before splitting, you will cause data leakage.]"""

